  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe goal is to minimize the overlap integral $C_5$ for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. We use differential evolution (DE) to search for an optimal step function h over discrete grid representations. The optimization is performed on several grid sizes (70 to 1400) to escape local optima, with resolution doubling at each stage. Convergence is monitored using a Bayesian regret lower bound, and we use interpolation to transfer solutions between grids. Multi-start procedures ensure diversity.\n\nKey bottlenecks addressed:\n1. Nonconvexity - DE handles large-scale optimization without gradients.\n2. Dimensionality - Coarse-to-fine grids balance complexity and evaluation cost.\n3. Sum constraint - Monotonic interpolation preserves integral properties.\n\nThe central idea is that energy minimizers for overlapping functions exhibit geometric regularity. By exploring increasingly finer grids with simulated annealing at each stage, we identify both local patterns and global structure improvements.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nVariables:\n- $h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1]$ approximated by $n_{\\text{points}}$ grid points.\n- Let $dx = 2/n_{\\text{points}}$, $\\vec{h}$ the grid values.\n- $C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx = \\max(\\text{conv}) * dx$\n- $\\text{conv} = np.correlate(\\vec{h}, 1-\\vec{h}, \\text{mode=\"full\"})$\n\nConstraints:\n- $0 \\leq \\vec{h}[i] \\leq 1$ element-wise\n- $\\sum \\vec{h}[i] = n_{\\text{points}}/2$ (integral=1)\n\nOptimization:\n- Minimize $C_5$ subject to constraints.\n- Allowed discrete spaces $\\mathbb{R} \\to [0,1]^{n}$ with $n=70,140,280,...,1400$.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with grid of size 70\n   - Use DE with bounds=[(0,1)] * n\n   - Simulated annealing with M=75 per temperature level\n   - Maintain archive of pheromones for smooth moves\n\n2. Evaluation procedure:\n   - Compute convexity of objective via full correlation\n   - Use moving average for gradient approximation\n   - Apply 3-point Hermite interpolation for rank-1 perturbations\n\n3. Grid progression from *n* to *2n*:\n   - Linear interpolation preserving flux\n   - Non-oscillatory resampling\n   - Temperature schedule scales with log(n)\n\n4. Termination:\n   - Time budget check every 100 evaluations\n   - Warm restart at 50% improvement\n   - Fallback to simplex projection if stuck\n\n5. Validation:\n   - Re-evaluate candidates via parent's convolver\n   - Maintain separate storage for all visited states\n   - Use R\u00e9nyi entropy to detect overfitting\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n\n- Base configuration:\n   - Base grid: 70\n   - Max grid: 1400\n   - Temperature schedule: T(t) = 1000 / (1 + t/20)\n   - DE parameters: F=0.8, CR=0.7\n\n- Adaptive strategies upon no improvement:\n   - Trigger on >100 evaluations without improvement\n   - Apply coarse grid annealing\n   - Increase grid resolution while cooling\n\n- Progressive optimization:\n   - Phase 1: Coarse grid (70) exploration\n   - Phase 2: Medium grid (280) refinement\n   - Phase 3: Fine grid (500+) exploration\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n\n- Dominant cost: Grid doubling at ~50ms/n\n- Total evaluations:~1000 main grid + checkpoint=6e4\n- Estimated runtime: 1.4 million operations\n- BriEmployee optimization at bottleNECK: divide grid by 2 when convergence stalls\n- Fallback: Uniform random initialization at grid=70\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\n- Feasibility checks:\n  1. $0 \\leq \\vec{h}[i] \\leq 1$ for all i\n  2. $\\lvert\\sum \\vec{h}[i] - n/2\\rvert < 1$\n  3. Finite positive integral (conv error ~1e-6)\n\n- Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Re-evaluation: Uses parent's convolver for final candidate\n\nThe solution explicitly tracks (h, x, grid) tuples and uses hierarchical interpolation to blend solutions between scales, enabling both local refinement and global exploration.",
